{:edb/db-before-t 333 :edb/db-after-t 334 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533646 50 #inst "2026-10-04T06:03:48.789Z" 13194139533646 true] [17592186110525 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243846 13194139533646 true] [17592186110525 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243853 13194139533646 true] [17592186110525 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243922 13194139533646 true] [17592186110525 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243929 13194139533646 true] [17592186110525 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243936 13194139533646 true] [17592186110526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243866 13194139533646 true] [17592186110526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243873 13194139533646 true] [17592186110526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243915 13194139533646 true] [17592186110527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243860 13194139533646 true] [17592186110527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243863 13194139533646 true] [17592186110527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243887 13194139533646 true] [17592186110527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243894 13194139533646 true] [17592186110527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243901 13194139533646 true] [17592186110528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243825 13194139533646 true] [17592186110528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243832 13194139533646 true] [17592186110528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243839 13194139533646 true] [17592186110528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243880 13194139533646 true] [17592186110528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243908 13194139533646 true] [17592186111111 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533646 true] [17592186111111 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+5y=10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y & =10 \\\\ 5y & =-x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=5x-4$ has a slope of $5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot 5=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=5x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line." 13194139533646 true] [17592186111112 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533646 true] [17592186111112 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $12x-4y=5,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-4y & =5 \\\\ -4y & =-12x+5 \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $3.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ is perpendicular to the given line." 13194139533646 true] [17592186243825 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243826 13194139533646 true] [17592186243825 1089 "q-110140" 13194139533646 true] [17592186243825 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533646 true] [17592186243825 1100 "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$" 13194139533646 true] [17592186243825 1113 #uuid "88e2feea-9e2f-596d-a324-3c357fe60543" 13194139533646 true] [17592186243825 1138 false 13194139533646 true] [17592186243825 1145 "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(1,4),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $4.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=4.$" 13194139533646 true] [17592186243826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243827 13194139533646 true] [17592186243826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243828 13194139533646 true] [17592186243826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243829 13194139533646 true] [17592186243826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243830 13194139533646 true] [17592186243826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243831 13194139533646 true] [17592186243826 1040 #edb/